Implementation is limited to the approved release/probe/compatibility owners and necessarily coupled regression tests and projections. New helper files belong under the existing release/native-probe modules; they do not create a second policy, release decision or governance writer.

### Ordered execution and tool routes

The following new tooling routes are planned deliverables of T-003/T-007, not claims that they already exist. Keep `scripts/native-probes/release-evidence.mjs` as their common entrypoint:

- `candidate --output <manifest.json>`: recompute the complete versioned candidate inventory and payload identities after preparation; output contains no credentials or qualified-state decision.
- `handoff --candidate <manifest.json> --output <directory>`: export checksummed test instructions, candidate metadata and evidence templates. Include exact source commit and validated package inventories; do not silently resolve latest or substitute another checkout.
- `preflight --candidate <manifest.json> --surface <surface> --model <model> --output <tuple.json>`: on the executor, capture and verify exact runtime/host/app/OS identities and required prerequisites. The Windows executor provides the observed model selection; reject empty/current/latest placeholders and freeze the returned tuple before the first native case.
- `desktop-template --candidate <manifest.json> --tuple <tuple.json> --output <record.json>`: produce an unverified Desktop Code observation template, never a completed pass. The operator must supply actual references and observations for session, upgrade and recovery.
- `import --bundle <directory> --candidate <manifest.json> --review <review.json>`: inspect referenced local files and their digests, validate tuple/candidate/case coverage and the reviewed bundle binding, then import accepted redacted records and update native manifest references. Reject invalid input without partial publication. A review file records cooperative evidence review; it grants no AGDF approval.
- `validate --candidate <manifest.json> --directory <directory> --all-hosts`: evaluate the complete policy-required host matrix. Preserve the existing Codex `--directory` route for its scoped workflow, with unchanged exact release-SHA safeguards; it must not count as all-host qualification.

Both CLI probes gain `--candidate <manifest.json> --tuple <tuple.json> --scenario <case>` with case values `dispatch`, `failure_case`, `skill_continuation`, `upgrade`, `recovery`. The Codex workflow may derive its exact tuple from the pinned canonical projection. All new script arguments are parsed as data, not shell text or commands embedded in observations.

After implementation checks, T-009 uses this order: `npm ci --ignore-scripts`; `npm run set-version -- 0.20.0` with the canonical release transaction and registry preflight; `npm run build:prepare`; `npm --prefix packages/mcp-server ci --ignore-scripts`; `npm run compatibility:record`; `npm run build`; relevant targeted tests; `npm run verify:ci`. If the source profile contract needs explicit digest acceptance, first inspect the actual planned contract diff and retain its exact reviewed digest; never weaken history checks or manufacture a historical tag. A payload-budget failure requires a measured scoped assessment through its existing owner, not an arbitrary allowance increase.

Generation and tests consuming generated outputs run sequentially. Independent read-only tests may be batched only after their shared preparation has completed. The full shared plan remains authoritative; do not replace it with just the targeted tests listed below. New native/evidence regression tests must be wired into the maintained npm/verification/CI inventory so they run on ordinary PRs without host credentials. Refresh source-dependent evidence after the final source change, before final candidate freezing.

### Native scope, cases and prerequisites

Codex has ten required platform/scenario observations: five cases on native Linux and five on native Windows. Common prerequisites/steps remain host identity, deliberate install consent, control fixture, discovery and owned removal; scenario-specific evidence must retain the existing structured checks. Upgrade/recovery records include the released baseline or supported damaged state and fresh-session loaded identity. Jobs retain current timeout bounds; capture actual elapsed time and fail an overrun instead of raising the limit or dropping tests.

Claude CLI has five corresponding native Windows cases. The Desktop Code surface supplies three independent observation records: fresh coding-session access, post-upgrade access and post-recovery access. Each record identifies the actual app version, session, candidate and tested access path, with the primary references required by the SD. Do not turn the five CLI records into Desktop proof. Use a safely isolated Desktop test profile or dedicated test environment verified on that computer; if the app cannot safely expose the candidate and its identity, report unverified and stop that surface.

Before native execution: candidate inputs must be finalized and clean; test executors must have the required installed hosts, allowed model and legitimate existing authentication; CI requires the existing protected environment and `CODEX_API_KEY`. Credentials and executable access are never inferred from TP approval. A missing prerequisite stops its scenario and records the named owner/action. It does not stop independent authorized implementation work or close any qualification requirement. No new account, purchase, credential transfer, remote access, thread messaging or publishing is authorized by this plan.

The external Windows test instructions and templates are prepared here; their execution and return remain with the other computer. No local Claude execution is required. Exact installed versions/model are captured by that preflight before launch, not guessed from the local macOS clients or the user's word current. Candidate or tuple mismatch requires a new matching handoff/execution, never editing a completed observation to make it match.

