## Planning Decisions

| Decision | Source | Status | Resolution | Owner |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Resolve concrete execution detail | SD: Concrete cases, runtime tuple and bounded commands | resolved | T-003/T-007 implement the explicit candidate/handoff/preflight/import/validate routes; T-010 requires ten Codex, five Windows Claude CLI and three independent Desktop records. Capture/freeze actual Windows versions/model before execution; prerequisites, evidence and stop actions are assigned per scenario above. No runtime tuple is guessed or missing access acquired by implication | TP author; Windows test executor; Codex native CI executor |
| Sequence and candidate freezing | TP | resolved | T-001 preparation precedes source changes; generation precedes dependent checks; full verification precedes frozen candidate/handoff; native release-grade results require the final authorized clean source and matching content identity | Codex release owner |
| Baseline and failure case | TP | resolved | Use released 0.14.5 with integrity verification; inject a supported owned stale/incomplete install, repair only through production owner, preserve foreign sentinels and capture fresh loaded identity and cleanup | Codex lifecycle owner |
| Bounded execution | TP | resolved | Existing native job maximum remains 10 minutes; model sessions remain individually bounded, existing scenarios retained, elapsed times recorded; an overrun is a failure requiring scoped partitioning, never a larger timeout | Codex CI owner |
| External execution prerequisites | TP | resolved | The user identified a separate Windows computer with Claude Desktop and CLI. Verify actual versions/model/isolation/auth during preflight at T-010, because those installed values can change before execution. This does not alter matrix or acceptance; unavailable prerequisites stop the affected scenario and remain explicit evidence obligations | Windows test executor; Codex evidence reviewer |
| Evidence, VCS and publication boundaries | TP | resolved | Prepare reviewable files and Windows instructions locally. No autonomous remote access, messaging, credential transfer, commit/push/tag, package publication or marketplace action; a clean shared ref requires its own authorized VCS handoff if not already available. Record the prerequisite rather than faking final-SHA evidence | Codex release owner; AGDF maintainer |

Runtime values are intentionally observed at their execution resolution point, with fixed owners and failure actions, because the installed remote versions and final candidate do not yet exist as tested facts. There is no unresolved product or design choice delegated to the executor. If execution reveals a material change to accepted scope, design or ownership, stop the affected work and route clarification to the earliest approved source; do not silently revise that source or reinterpret the criteria.

## 4. Brownfield Scope

T-001 rechecks the approved UR/PRD/SD and completed Brownfield Review, current exact run/revision, source and index baseline, canonical plugin definition/schema, version/profile/history checks, provenance/hash helpers, source/payload inventory and generator boundaries, native probes and session helper, Windows host-command resolution, publication graph and verification inventory, compatibility imports/rendering, and the corresponding existing tests. Its output is a run-local BROWNFIELD_ANALYSIS.md recorded through the existing internal-step owner before CD+Tests. Escalate any conflicting ownership or new source-intent effect rather than writing a parallel policy or runtime path.

Preserve the unrelated `core-model-handoff-simplification-20261015-01` artefact directory and any later foreign work. Do not reset, clean, stage or overwrite foreign paths to obtain a clean candidate. Review the actual prepared scope and use an authorized clean candidate/ref when final native evidence is needed. Run-specific approval/presentation files remain under the existing control writers; implementation never hand-edits their seals or approvals.

## 5. Out Of Scope

Publishing, release tags, marketplace submission, automatic VCS operations, new remote services, proactive thread messages, blanket credential/tool approval, changing Core gate authority, redesigning product UI, qualifying/removing experimental hosts, weakening payload/history/native checks, increasing test timeouts or omitting coverage. No current native result, clean release SHA, remote tuple or completed human acceptance is asserted by this plan.

## 6. Risks And Blockers

The current source has uncommitted release artefacts and unrelated work; it is not already a release-grade clean commit. Final native execution depends on an authorized clean candidate and available native runners/credentials. The external Windows machine must return actual CLI and separate Desktop records. Those facts are execution evidence obligations, not permission to manufacture results, modify another daily-use profile or claim this preparation completed qualification.

Stop the affected work on unknown ownership, unsafe baseline extraction, manifest coverage gaps, changed candidate/tuple, missing primary references, secret leakage, unsupported Desktop isolation, failed cleanup or timeout. Preserve useful independent work. If required safe execution cannot be obtained under the approved contract, QA remains revise/block with the exact missing obligation. Any product/design change follows the existing source-revision owner and renewed approval, never a silent exception in the harness. Current reports distinguish preparation, native qualification, human acceptance and publication throughout.

## 7. Next Step

Present this exact plan for `Approval: TP`. Approval permits implementation-preparation Brownfield Analysis, followed by the scoped implementation and checks when that analysis supports them. Record implementation/test results through the existing CD+Tests owner, then required reviews and QA. Missing external results may leave readiness pending; deliberate QA/UAT/OR and separate publication/VCS boundaries remain in force.

## AGDF Approval Summary (de; source=en)

- Ziel: Die freigegebene 0.20.0-Vorbereitung mit nachvollziehbaren Aufgaben, Prüfungen und externen Host-Nachweisen umsetzen.
- Plan: Elf Aufgaben decken Bestandsprüfung, kanonische Host-Policy, Kandidatenbindung, Upgrade/Wiederherstellung, beide Probes, Testübergabe/Import, Release-Sperre, Dokumentation, Verifikation und Reviews ab.
- Abdeckung: 28 Szenarien ordnen alle acht PRD-Kriterien und acht SD-Entscheidungen konkreten Aufgaben, erwarteten Ergebnissen und Nachweisen zu. Copilot/OpenCode bleiben experimentell; ihre Tests bleiben erhalten.
- Native Prüfungen: Für Codex sind zehn Linux-/Windows-Fälle geplant. Vom anderen Windows-Rechner kommen fünf Claude-CLI-Fälle und drei getrennte Desktop-Coding-Beobachtungen. Exakte Versions-/Modellwerte und sichere Testbereiche werden dort vor dem Start geprüft.
- Ablauf: Nach TP-Freigabe folgt zuerst die Implementierungsvorbereitung, dann Quelländerungen, Versionspflege auf 0.20.0, Paket-/Regressionstests und ein eingefrorener Kandidat mit Windows-Testpaket. Erst passende native Ergebnisse schließen die Qualifikation.
- Grenzen: Zugangsdaten und Rechnerzugriff werden nicht vorausgesetzt oder übertragen. Fehlender sauberer Release-Stand, Runner, Authentifizierung oder Windows-Belege bleiben offene Ausführungspflichten. Keine automatischen Commits, Pushes, Tags, Veröffentlichung oder Marketplace-Einreichung.
- Abschluss: Pflichtnachweise, unveränderte Zeitlimits und alle bisherigen Prüfungen bleiben verbindlich. Reviews und QA bewerten die tatsächlichen Ergebnisse; ein vorbereitetes Paket allein ist keine Auslieferungsfreigabe.
